shgrep portable package (Windows x64, @SHGREP_HS_ARCH@)

Keep shgrep.exe, shgrep-compiler.dll and shgrep.dat from the same build together.
The compiler DLL contains Clang, LLVM and LLD; the executable loads it only for
REPL workers. Ordinary search, MCP startup and --license do not load it.
shgrep.dat holds only Clang's builtin x86 headers, compressed; they are read without
extraction. Dependency notices are plain files in licenses\, this guide is README.txt
and the optional LSP download script is tools\fetch_lsp.py. MSVC and Windows SDK headers
and libraries are discovered on the target PC and are not bundled.
Each C++ worker writes generated objects and DLLs into a private temporary session
directory. DLLs remain loaded for the worker's lifetime; the parent removes the
directory after confirming worker exit. Crashes or failed deletion can leave files.
Windows system libraries remain OS dependencies.

C++ REPL requirements:
  Install Visual Studio or Build Tools with Desktop development with C++ and a
  Windows 10/11 SDK. The MSVC toolset must match the build's major.minor family
  (@SHGREP_MSVC_VERSION@); servicing patch versions may differ.
  A compatible x64 Visual C++ Redistributable must also be installed. Cells use
  the shared runtime; shgrep.exe and shgrep-compiler.dll each retain a static runtime.
  Only scalar values and caller-owned bytes cross the compiler DLL entry point.
  Discovery uses the developer environment first, then Visual Studio Setup
  Configuration. The SDK is found through its environment settings or registry.
  To select installations explicitly, set VCToolsInstallDir, WindowsSdkDir and
  WindowsSDKVersion in the environment inherited by the MCP server.
  Missing or incompatible resources produce a C++ worker startup error.
  Semantic navigation through the REPL has the same requirements.
  Direct filesystem and text tools do not require these REPL resources.
  Native TLS belongs to each generated DLL. Cross-cell accesses use owner
  accessors, preserving per-thread storage and initialization.
  One execution thread persists across cells. Normal reset drains the native
  CRT TLS destructor lists, then cell globals, on that same thread before it exits.
  Generated DLL callback forwarding suppresses repeated CRT TLS destruction;
  other TLS notifications retain their normal delivery. Callback metadata is
  prepared before Windows loads each DLL, with image/ASLR checks. Unsupported
  CRT callback layouts fail explicitly. This integration remains experimental.
  Normal reset requests cooperative shutdown, with a one-second grace period.
  Timeouts and stuck shutdowns terminate the worker; destructors are not guaranteed.
  Link/load or cell-initialization failure also resets the affected session.
  Errors carry the compiler's and linker's messages. To debug the DLL executor itself, set
  SHGREP_REPL_EMISSION_DIAGNOSTICS=1: link failures and worker crashes then also include LLVM IR
  snapshots of the cell's DLL (up to a few hundred kilobytes), and every cell records them.

Start the MCP STDIO server for a repository:
  C:\tools\shgrep\shgrep.exe --root C:\work\my-repo
The first root is the workspace: only it gets a live search index, searches without a path
cover only it, and its language servers start in the background (SHGREP_LSP_WARMUP=0 turns
that off). Other roots are searched when named. Index and server start-up progress goes to
.shgrep\index.log in the workspace (rewritten at each start), not into tool results. A drive
root or user profile is never indexed; its log is %LOCALAPPDATA%\shgrep\index.log.
SHGREP_INDEX_MAX_FILES (default 500000, 0 for no limit) stops the index for larger trees.
.shignore (created empty in the workspace at start) lists what shgrep skips in search, file
listing and its index, in .gitignore syntax. It is read after .gitignore and .ignore, so its
rules win, and it works in any folder like .gitignore. Language servers keep their own rules.
The MCP client starts shgrep.exe. REPL sessions use isolated child processes of
that same executable; each worker loads shgrep-compiler.dll from the executable
directory and retains it until process exit. Missing or incompatible compiler
DLLs produce a worker startup error. The internal worker and data-packer modes
should not be launched as an MCP server.

Example MCP client entry (replace both paths):
  command = 'C:\tools\shgrep\shgrep.exe'
  args = ['--root', 'C:\work\my-repo']

Regular search:
  C:\tools\shgrep\shgrep.exe search "TODO|FIXME" --root C:\work\my-repo

Print the dependency notices in licenses\:
  C:\tools\shgrep\shgrep.exe --license

LSP servers are separate downloads. Run tools\fetch_lsp.py from this package on an
internet-connected PC:
  python tools\fetch_lsp.py C:\shared\shgrep
Transfer its resulting lsp folder alongside the three core files for offline use,
or set SHGREP_LSP_ROOT to its parent directory. .shgrep/lsp.json can add or override
servers. The downloader and any dependencies of those language servers are
separate from shgrep's core files and C++ runtime dependencies.

Repository dependencies, generated headers, compile databases, language toolchains
and package caches must be available for semantic analysis of the target project.
Native C++ cells run with the Windows user's permissions.

Requirements: Windows 8 or later, x64 and a CPU supported by this @SHGREP_HS_ARCH@ build.
Microsoft compiler and Windows SDK resources retain their original license terms.
The ZIP contains shgrep.exe, shgrep-compiler.dll, shgrep.dat, README.txt,
tools\fetch_lsp.py and licenses\, with a separate .sha256 checksum.
